# A name-keyed Python builtin taken as a VALUE must mean OUR builtin, even when
# an imported Pascal RTL unit declares a routine of the same name.
#
# Its own file rather than a row in the sibling fixture because `import math` is
# a MODULE-WIDE fact: it changes what every name in the file resolves to, so
# folding it in would have altered the rows that are about something else.
#
# WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE BUG FROM "abs is not a value". Without the import,
# `f = abs` was a clean compile error -- loud, and nobody's data was wrong. WITH
# it, `abs` resolved to lib/rtl/math's `Abs(x: Integer)` (declared first) and was
# called through the Variant ABI. Measured 2026-09-13 at 9393ab277:
#
#     import math; f = abs; print(f(-5))                -> an EMPTY LINE, exit 0
#     import math; f = abs; print(f(-5)); print(f(-5.5)) -> SIGSEGV
#
# So an innocuous import two lines away turned a refusal into a silent wrong
# value and then a crash. `abs(-5)` spelled as a CALL was correct throughout,
# because the CALL path already has an explicit "own language first, and it
# overrules import order" rule -- the value path never got it. That is what
# makes the ORDER in PyBuiltinValueNameAhead load-bearing: the table is
# consulted BEFORE FindProcExactCase, not after.
#
# The float row is the one that segfaulted, so it must stay: an int-only fixture
# passes on a build where `abs` binds to math's Abs(Integer).
#
# Valid CPython; .expected captured from the oracle.

import math

f = abs
print(f(-5))
print(f(-5.5))
print(list(map(abs, [-5, -5.5])))

# the call form, which was correct all along -- here so a regression that takes
# BOTH is attributable to the name-keyed intercepts rather than to this fix
print(abs(-5), abs(-5.5))

# math itself still reachable and unshadowed by any of this
print(math.floor(2.7))
